diff --git a/docs/privacy-audit.md b/docs/privacy-audit.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..acfc005
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/privacy-audit.md
@@ -0,0 +1,115 @@
+# Privacy review and operating requirements
+
+Review date: 2026-10-16. Scope: application source, dependency advisories,
+SSR output, and automated HTTP integration checks. This is an internal technical
+review, not an independent audit or compliance certification. No browser or HAR
+verification was performed.
+
+## Integration inventory and decisions
+
+| Integration | Previous exposure | Decision |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Cloud Functions analytics client | Unversioned JavaScript on every page, including credentials pages | Removed; no analytics replacement or telemetry collection |
+| Cloud Functions demo login/register API | Username and password sent directly from the browser | Removed; account pages explain unavailability and collect no credentials |
+| Advertising SDK / global queues | Arbitrary third-party script could consume slot queues | Removed dormant queues; components render nothing |
+| Google Fonts CSS, font files and preconnects | Visitor IP, browser/network metadata | Removed; system serif stack |
+| Imgur and CinemAI images | IP, referrer and potential third-party credentials | Replaced with existing first-party assets; credentialed image requests removed |
+| developer.chrome.com | Explicit outbound navigation | Retained ordinary link; no embedded SDK or preconnect; no-referrer policy |
+| Hosting provider | Network information necessary for delivery | Required; deployment operator must review access logs, subprocessors, region, retention and backups |
+| npm build dependencies | Build/supply-chain access, no intended visitor data transfer | Lockfile installs and advisory audit; review each dependency update |
+
+`pnpm audit --prod` found 0 known advisories on the review date. This does not
+prove a dependency or service has no vulnerabilities or tracking behavior.
+
+## Data inventory and lifetime
+
+Reading requires no session creation. Bookmarking creates a random first-party
+session identifier with HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, a scoped path, a one-day maximum
+age, and Secure in production. Server data contains at most 50 known article IDs.
+There are no account records, tokens, browser identifiers, reading timestamps,
+fingerprints, consent preferences, analytics events or marketing profiles.
+
+The custom driver retains sessions only in process memory. It removes data on a
+timer 24 hours after the last update, checks expiry on reads, and deletes data
+immediately on deletion or removal of the last bookmark. Reads do not renew
+retention. Restarting the process loses lists. At 10,000 sessions, the oldest
+stored list is evicted to bound memory use. Do not deploy multiple instances
+with this driver: use an approved shared store with enforced TTL, encryption at
+rest, access restrictions and deletion guarantees first. The former filesystem
+driver must not be restored without scheduled physical deletion of expired data.
+
+Exports include the only application data retained for the current browser:
+article IDs. Deletion destroys server state and expires the exact scoped cookie;
+replaying it cannot recover a deleted list. Origin-wide Clear-Site-Data is not used
+because this application lives under a path and may share its origin with other
+applications. There is no authentication/logout state to clear.
+
+When migrating an existing deployment, the operator must purge legacy
+`.astro/sessions` and `.astro/session` records and coordinate deletion of data
+previously transmitted to the removed services. Changing the application cannot
+retroactively remove those third-party records. Legacy `plant_stats_cache_*`
+cookies scoped to `/api/plant-stats` are no longer written or used; expire those
+at that legacy path at the edge if this demo was previously deployed.
+
+## Enforcement and deployment
+
+Astro generates hash-based script/style CSP in the response header; middleware
+preserves that header. The policy blocks external connections, images, fonts,
+frames, plugins and base tags, permits only same-origin form actions, and uses
+strict-dynamic for framework hydration. ClientRouter was removed so navigation
+uses a fresh document and its own CSP. Trusted Types enforcement is deferred:
+Astro's React renderer uses `template.innerHTML` for slots; enforcing it without
+adapting that renderer risks broken hydration. No permissive Trusted Types policy
+has been introduced. CSP telemetry is not collected by this application.
+
+Middleware rejects cross-site API requests and requires the exact request origin
+for mutations, including browsers without Fetch Metadata. Public page navigation
+remains available. Reading-list writes validate MIME type, bounded streaming body
+size, JSON shape, known article ID and list limits before saving. Private JSON and
+pages containing bookmark state use no-store. Referrers are suppressed, unused
+powerful permissions are disabled, and clickjacking, MIME sniffing, opener and
+resource isolation policies are enforced.
+
+Serve production through HTTPS. Start HSTS at 300 seconds; increase only after
+validating HTTPS routing. Do not add includeSubDomains or preload until all
+subdomains are ready. Mirror security headers at the edge for static files,
+redirects and errors that bypass Astro middleware. Confirm the trusted proxy
+passes the original scheme/host correctly; do not trust arbitrary forwarded
+headers from public clients. Restrict access to application memory/processes.
+
+Do not log raw request bodies, cookies, full URLs/query strings, authentication
+headers, reading-list values or identifiers. Configure infrastructure access-log
+redaction, least privilege, short retention and backup expiry before production.
+The application policy explicitly distinguishes these operator-controlled logs.
+
+## Repeatable review
+
+Run before release and whenever adding or changing a third party:
+
+```
+pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
+pnpm audit:privacy
+pnpm audit --prod
+pnpm build
+pnpm test:privacy
+```
+
+The source audit rejects new literal external origins, direct browser storage,
+fingerprinting and telemetry APIs. It is a regression guard, not a complete
+network audit: dynamic URLs, dependency code and infrastructure require review.
+The HTTP suite verifies actual headers, inline script hashes, no anonymous
+cookies, credential-free account pages, CSRF rejection, malformed/oversized
+bodies, isolated lists, export and deletion. It does not emulate browser CSP
+execution or establish third-party compliance.
+
+Re-review quarterly and on dependency/integration changes. For every proposed
+service, record an owner, purpose, exact fields transferred, destination origins,
+retention/deletion mechanism, subprocessor policy and approval date here before
+adding its origin to the source audit or CSP. Default to no integration until
+reviewed. For optional embeds, use a first-party facade and an explicit action
+before any third-party request. For immutable third-party code, require SRI;
+avoid unversioned remote scripts. Reintroducing authentication requires an
+approved backend with verified password handling, secure session termination,
+rate limiting and automated account access/deletion, not browser token storage.
+Arrange an independent audit before handling sensitive production accounts;
+record the auditor, scope, date, findings and remediation evidence here when done.
